{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 1, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "**Raymond III Count of Tripoli**\n\nRaymond III was Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187. He was the son of Raymond II of Tripoli and Hodierna of Rethel. Raymond III became count at a young age following the assassination of his father in 1152. His mother Hodierna served as regent until he came of age. He was a key figure in the Crusader states during the 12th century.\n\n*highlighted section part 1*\n\n**Early Life and Reign**\n\nRaymond III was born around 1140. His father Raymond II was assassinated in 1152 by the Hashashin. Hodierna of Rethel his mother ruled Tripoli as regent until Raymond III reached adulthood. He was knighted in 1164 and began participating in military campaigns. Raymond III was captured at the Battle of Harim in 1164 and held captive by Nur ad-Din for three years. After his release he returned to Tripoli and continued to govern his county.\n\n**Political and Military Involvement**\n\nRaymond III played a significant role in the politics of the Crusader states. He was a strong supporter of the Byzantine Empire and maintained close ties with Emperor Manuel I Komnenos. Raymond III also had a complicated relationship with King Amalric I of Jerusalem. He supported Amalric but later clashed with him over the succession of the throne. Raymond III was a key figure in the Battle of Montgisard in 1177 where he fought alongside Baldwin IV against Saladin. He was also involved in the dispute over the succession to the throne after Baldwin IVs death in 1185.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*\n\n**Succession and Legacy**\n\nRaymond III became regent for Baldwin V of Jerusalem in 1185 after the death of Baldwin IV. He ruled alongside Joscelin III of Edessa. Raymond III worked to secure the succession of Baldwin V but the young king died in 1186. After Baldwins death Raymond III supported Humphrey IV of Toron against Guy of Lusignan for the throne. Humphrey married Isabella I of Jerusalem but Raymond IIIs candidate lost. Raymond III died in 1187 and was succeeded by his cousin Raymond IV of Tripoli.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*\n\nRaymond III was a prominent figure in the Crusader states. He navigated complex political and military challenges during his reign. His actions had lasting impacts on the region and he is remembered as a significant leader in the history of the Crusades.", "logprobs": [{"token": "**", "logprob": -0.16611935198307037}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.5445922613143921}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -2.3841855067985307e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.00016175392374861985}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.03488249331712723}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.00010918975021922961}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -1.0132738680113107e-05}, {"token": "**\n\n", "logprob": -0.48084405064582825}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.039470527321100235}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -1.156323378381785e-05}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.0004070168943144381}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.21503284573554993}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.2742631435394287}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -1.0728830375228426e-06}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -5.364403477869928e-06}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0005395148764364421}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.0006276782951317728}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -1.4781842764932662e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -0.00016652150952722877}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -0.000742279109545052}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -0.00018690270371735096}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -0.5760633945465088}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.006745306774973869}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -8.940656698541716e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -4.589452510117553e-05}, {"token": "8", "logprob": -4.708655978902243e-05}, {"token": "7", "logprob": -4.994744449504651e-05}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.031241102144122124}, {"token": " He", "logprob": -0.010986315086483955}, {"to